detail.abstract:
The model this paper presents resolves the long-standing ambiguity between ends and means in war. 
<br>
 &nbsp;For over two centuries, war has relied on an unstable separation of tactics, operations, and strategy, each presumed to possess its own logic. Yet this hierarchical division reliably collapses whenever harsh friction intervenes-disrupting plans, generating uncontrolled chaos, and often yielding advantages to the opponent. As these categories are ambiguously defined , they have historically generated persistent confusion both for headquarters and their subordinate units. They are, in fact, merely scale-variants of a single adaptive system. I propose this model to demonstrate that war can be understood as a single adaptive decision making system, thereby resolving the two dilemmas at once. If adopted, this framework would fundamentally reorganize how war is theorized and analyzed.&nbsp;
